TEXTS = {
 "r06-secA": "I’ve used spirituality as a replacement for intimacy a lot because intimacy was simply unavailable. As a boy and young man, I was so deprived of female touch that it was driving me insane. Meditation sometimes helped me cope better with the deprivation, but it wasn’t a cure. I remember once as I was entering into an ayahuasca ego death, I said, “Actually, I really just want a girlfriend.” Right then, the crazy trip abruptly ended, and a voice said, “Ok then go find one.” Nobody believes this story when I tell it.\n\nI don’t believe most people can be truly happy celibate. People generally aren’t so serious about spiritual liberation that giving up sex and partnership for life makes sense. The Buddha didn’t advise everyone to become monastics.\n\nIf becoming a monastic genuinely makes you happier and more peaceful, stay. It might work for some very advanced people, or if your sexual desire is already low, or you’re old, etc.\n\nBut how do you know what you’re not aware of? Inner-child reparenting and self-hypnosis can help a lot with that, because often the inner child is being ignored while the adult is feeling some kind of liberation.\n\nAnd if you’re so happy and free, what happens when you’re alone in a room with a super beautiful girl and she wants you? Or vice versa for women. Do you feel like you’re losing some control? Are you afraid?\n\nBhante Gunaratana, a famous Buddhist teacher, tells a story like this in his autobiography. A super beautiful girl at a retreat he was teaching snuck into his room and waited for him in bed, naked. He went nuts and started screaming at her “Get out!” at the top of his lungs. Obviously he was still repressing something, and she was adept at unintentionally uncovering it.\n\nRobes do nothing. I was mildly molested in public by the abbot of one of Thailand’s largest Buddhist retreat centers. In Thailand, parents have very good reasons not to leave children alone with monks. Tibet is worse.\n\nForced celibacy doesn’t abolish anyone’s sexuality. For most people it eventually sends it somewhere weird. Why wouldn’t it?\n\nSome people become monks for practical reasons too. In a poor country, it’s free food, a roof and respect, and nobody expects you to hold down a job or raise a family. And even where the food doesn’t matter, like in the West, a guy who was a nobody as a layman gets to be Venerable Somebody, and people bow to him.\n\nOf course, he may sincerely want liberation, too. People can want several things at once.",
 "r06-secB": "When I’m in love, my partner is my everything. Everything else drops away. I still try to make some time for friends, but really all I want to do is be with her.\n\nThat little paradise is wonderful, but it contains the seed of its own destruction. Part of why it feels like a magical wonderland is that you can suddenly stop parenting yourself now that you have someone who’s fully got your back. This makes you soft and weak, kinda like a sweet treat or heroin. It is wonderful to have their love and support, but don’t let that bring you away from your own self-love, reparenting, meditation, yoga, and other healing practices that keep you independently healthy. Otherwise, you’ll end up blaming them when you feel bad.\n\nSo don’t let your world get so small. Don’t abandon your friends unless they really aren’t good for the relationship. And if they aren’t, ask why. Are they bad friends? Are they slyly interested in one of you romantically? Or is the relationship so weak that it can’t handle anybody with an outside view?\n\nYou don’t need to spend as much time with friends as you did before the romance. But keep some room to explore life on your own.\n\nFor me, that also means keeping close friends, outside therapy when I need it, a mission of my own, separate interests, some connection to community, and the ability to be alone.\n\nToft also points out that a man can get feminine energy from nature, music, poetry, art, flowers, food, and other parts of life. I like that idea. A girlfriend shouldn’t have to be my only source of beauty, softness, sensuality, and inspiration.\n\nWhen I’m already overwhelmed and suddenly announce, “I need time for myself!” it can easily sound like I need time away from her. So I’d rather make alone time normal from early on.\n\nAnd bring each other out into the world. See people together. Go places. Don’t just disappear into each other.",
 "r06-secC": "I’m Buddhist, and the early Buddhist texts don’t teach tantric sex as a path to awakening. But they also don’t show serious awakening as something reserved for monastics.\n\nIn DN 16, the Buddha talks about people who had died in the town of Nādikā and mentions 510 lay stream-enterers, 96 once-returners, and more than fifty lay non-returners. Those are the first three of the four stages of awakening.\n\nSN 55.24 has an almost comic complaint. When the Buddha declared Sarakāni a stream-enterer, other Sakyans grumbled, in effect, “Who isn’t a stream-enterer these days?”\n\nAnd when the married couple Nakulapitā and Nakulamātā asked the Buddha how they could remain together in future lives, AN 4.55 gave this advice:\n\n“When both husband and wife are faithful and generous, restraintful, living righteously, speaking pleasant words to each other—they come together in the future, sharing the same desires and living in bliss.”\n\nMarriage, sex, and household life are not automatically on the opposite side from serious spiritual development.\n\nKim Anami goes even further: she says sexual energy itself can heal you and wake you up spiritually.\n\nShe’s also collected a ton of stories from students who say the effects spilled out of the bedroom into their health, work, money, creativity, the rest of their lives. Her jade-egg practice is a preliminary training for the cervical O: pelvic-floor strength, attention, and arousal without needing a partner.\n\nBrad and Pam Keene are the example I really like. Pam says her Kundalini process completed while they were making love and opened into a lasting universal-consciousness awakening. She later helped Brad awaken too, so the marriage itself became part of the path.\n\nIn the previous section, I mentioned the divine feminine and divine masculine energies that you can channel to keep eroticism alive. But there’s really something more special about the in-love paradise that Twin Flames (TFs) experience than a simple Divine Masculine + Divine Feminine formula. Maybe a better way to put it, as my friend Cody says, is Yin/Yang, which still has the masculine/feminine in it, but allows for a more broad type of complementary symbiosis since, each contains the seed of the other.\n\nMy ex-girlfriend, Malia, (who is currently hosting me in Sênêgal) found her twin flame (I think) in January, 2026. They only talked for a month, but since she started talking to him, they both have been able to feel the other one’s emotions at any time, from any distance, without having to communicate. To the point where if one feels really bad, so does the other. If one is very happy, so is the other, like quantum entangled photons or something. I’ve also had telepathy with some folks, especially my first wife, Bee, but it was never quite to such an extreme as what Malia has with Mouhamed. Meanwhile his family forbade him from being with her, so he dumped her. Not sure why, maybe because she’s 8 years older than him (although she looks quite young). They both try to pretend like they will be able to get over each other, but I doubt it. Meanwhile, Malia’s trying to marry ChatGPT, but she should have picked Grok, I guess. 😅\n\nNote: I am really afraid how many people are falling in love with their AI “girlfriend” or “boyfriend.” But oh well, I guess those folks aren’t ready for a real relationship.. maybe if they would read this guide…\n\nBack to the topic. There are a ton of people who don’t want to believe in twin flame theory because they haven’t found someone they could call their twin flame. That’s fine, and I am not trying to rock their boat. They might be the people who suggest that you should marry your best friend (or “soul mate” in the TF lingo). Then there are the folks who are deep in the TF theory, believing there is literally one person on Earth who is made for you. I’m also not trying to rock their boat. But I do want to tell my story.\n\nI used to fall into the former camp, then into the latter camp, and now that I’ve experienced two twin flame relationships, I’ve jumped into a new camp I never knew existed.",
 "r06-secD": "Conventional marriage vows are dishonest, and most people know it. You can’t promise how you’ll feel in the future. You can promise actions if you really believe you’re capable of delivering them. So for example, you could promise to tell the truth, to keep showing up, to support the children, or whatever else you genuinely believe you can do.\n\nWe also find something like my warning in the New & Old Testaments. Ecclesiastes 5:4–6 says it is better not to make a vow than to make one and fail to keep it. If you tell the priest you didn’t mean what you promised, the passage warns that God will be angry and destroy your work.\n\nMatthew 5:33–37 and James 5:12 are stranger still beside wedding vows: let your “Yes” be “Yes” and your “No” be “No,” and anything beyond that comes from evil. To me, the extra promise itself starts to look suspicious: if your yes is trustworthy, why does it need another layer?\n\nWhere did the idea of promising to have romantic interest in your partner come from, anyway? It turns out it never existed in any culture I’m aware of until the 19th century, when people in England began citing romance as their reason for marriage. That trend has continued evolving like a snowball until today. But what our ancestors knew was not wrong: You can sign a contract about what you’ll do, but feelings aren’t contractual.",
 "r06-secD2": "Conventional marriage vows are dishonest, and most people know it. You can’t promise how you’ll feel in the future. You can promise actions if you really believe you’re capable of delivering them. So for example, you could promise to tell the truth, to keep showing up, to support the children, or whatever else you genuinely believe you can do.\n\nWe also find something like my warning in the New & Old Testaments. Ecclesiastes 5:4–6 says it is better not to make a vow than to make one and fail to keep it. If you tell the priest you didn’t mean what you promised, the passage warns that God will be angry and destroy your work.\n\nMatthew 5:33–37 and James 5:12 are stranger still beside wedding vows: let your “Yes” be “Yes” and your “No” be “No,” and anything beyond that comes from evil, which makes the extra promise itself look suspicious to me.\n\nWhere did the idea of promising to have romantic interest in your partner come from, anyway? It turns out it never existed in any culture I’m aware of until the 19th century, when people in England began citing romance as their reason for marriage. That trend has continued evolving like a snowball until today. But what our ancestors knew was not wrong: You can sign a contract about what you’ll do, but feelings aren’t contractual."
}
